WEBVTT
TITLE Sequential Circuits — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A sequential circuit has memory. Its output depends on the inputs present now and on the state it stored before.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
So the description is a state table: for each present state and each input, the next state and the output.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
One flip-flop holds one bit, so n of them hold at most 2 to the power n states. The number of states is what fixes the number of flip-flops.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Moore puts the output on the state alone. Mealy puts it on the state and the input, so it can react inside the cycle instead of at the edge.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Cross-couple two NOR gates - each output into the other input - and the loop holds a value with no clock at all.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Set drives the output to one, reset drives it to zero, and with both inputs at zero the latch holds whatever it had.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Both inputs at one is not a fourth function. Both outputs go to zero, so the pair is no longer complementary and the state is not a state.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Release both inputs at once and the latch races: the pair never settles, so which value wins is decided by gate delays, not by logic.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Put one inverter between the two inputs and the forbidden combination becomes impossible: one data line, one enable line.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
While the enable is high the latch is transparent: the output follows the input, including every glitch that lands on it.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Two latches in series with opposite enables make a master-slave flip-flop: the first samples, the second holds, and only one of them is open at a time.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
One sentence holds the distinction: a latch is level-sensitive and transparent, a flip-flop is edge-triggered and samples once.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
JK keeps the two set and reset lines and repairs the forbidden combination: with both at one, the output toggles instead of fighting.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The characteristic equation Q next equals J and not-Q, or not-K and Q, is the whole four-row table in one line.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
A level-triggered JK with both inputs at one does not toggle once. While the clock is high it keeps toggling, and how many times depends on how wide the clock is.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Edge-triggering samples once per edge and ends the race. Tie J and K together and you have the T flip-flop: Q next equals Q exclusive-OR T.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Three numbers describe a flip-flop: clock-to-Q, how long after the edge the output moves; setup time; and hold time.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The setup constraint: one clock period must cover the launch delay, the longest logic path, and the capture setup time.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
So the maximum frequency is one over that sum, taken over the slowest register-to-register path - not the longest path in the drawing.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Hold is a different check and contains no clock period at all: the shortest path must not arrive too early. Slowing the clock cannot fix it.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
An n-bit ripple counter is n toggle stages in a chain. Each stage halves the frequency, so the last output has a period 2 to the n times the clock period.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
A 64-microsecond period at the last of four stages means a 4-microsecond clock: 250 kilohertz. That is GATE CSE 2025 Set 2, Question 34.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The ripple has a cost: the delays add down the chain, so the worst transition settles only after n propagation delays and the maximum clock is one over n times that delay.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
A synchronous counter clocks every flip-flop together and decides with logic: bit k toggles exactly when every lower bit is one. And a mod-N counter needs the ceiling of log base 2 of N flip-flops.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
A shift register is flip-flops in a line, each passing its bit to the next on every clock. n bits move one place per cycle.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Feed the last output back unchanged and you get a ring counter: one 1 circulates, so there are n states, and all zeros is a locked state it can never leave.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Feed back the complement and you get a Johnson counter: 2n states from n flip-flops, and all zeros is no longer a trap.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Four flip-flops from 0000 give 0, 8, 12, 14, 15, 7, 3, 1 and back to 0. That sequence is GATE CSE 2015 Set 1, Question 11.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The design flow is five steps and none of them is clever: state diagram, state table, state assignment, flip-flop inputs, minimised expressions.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
State assignment is a free choice. It does not change the behaviour, only the size of the circuit, so two designers can produce different expressions for the same machine.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
The excitation table turns "this bit must go from 0 to 1" into the input that does it. For D the input is the next state itself; for T it is the exclusive-OR of present and next.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The unused states are a decision, not an accident: treat them as don't-cares and the circuit is smaller, but check where they go.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Do not simulate in your head. Write one next-state equation per flip-flop, straight from the gate network, and nothing else.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Then tabulate: every present state in a column, its next state in the next column, for every input. The cycle appears as a repeat.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
From 11 with the T flip-flop driven by the other output and the D flip-flop fed from it, the states go 11, 01, 10, 11: a three-state cycle. That is GATE CSE 2017 Set 1, Question 33.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
The last step is the one people skip: list the states the cycle never visits and ask where they go.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Latch or flip-flop: level-sensitive and transparent, against edge-triggered and sampled once. Everything else in the topic follows from that line.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Ripple or synchronous: the ripple is cheaper and slower by a factor of n; the synchronous is one flip-flop delay plus whatever logic the toggle rule needs.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
The five traps: the forbidden SR input, race-around, hold mistaken for setup, an uninitialised ring counter, and an unused state that locks out.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
The rule: name the state, write the next-state function, then count the clock. Every sequential question is those three steps in that order.

